Here, ArrayList.Test, is a test project where you place the test class and we are performing some testing on the methods present in the ArrayList class, using PEX.

- PUT_SetContract : is a method that sets a given value at the specified index;
- PUT_InsertContract : is a method that inserts value at a specified index;
- PUT_AddContract : is a method that adds value to the list;
- PUT_RemoveContract : is a method that removes item from list;
- PUT_GetContract : is a method that gets the value at the specified index.

Put methods represent different scenarios.

Each method takes pre-conditions under test and post-conditions under test (conditions of the method under test after applying the inputs). We then formulate a contract for the method under test and provide the pre-condition under test, inputs, and the post-conditions under test.

In our test setup for each method, we first record the pre-condition under test (count, indexOf(value), lastIndexOf(value), contains(value)) - this is all the important aspects that a method under test will change and we need to record. Then, execute the method under test and compute all the post-condition under test (count,indexOf(value), lastIndexOf(value), contains(value)). If they match with recorded pre-conditions, then our test setup is fine, else we've some mistake/bug in the method under test.

This is how we can use PEX for method under test contract testing.

It could be overkill for small methods, but when working with larger methods, PEX can be immensely helpful in discovering test cases where the method does "wrong".

Remember this is just a simple representation, actual working of PEX is way more complex and involves a deep understanding of state explosion, input reduction etc.

P.S. Since actual PEX tests may take quite a bit of time depending on the complexity of the class under test, you may need to run the test project locally or in a controlled environment where resources aren't constrained. However, the beauty of PEX is that it’s an automated tool that reduces the need for developers to write and maintain test cases themselves. If bugs are found, the tool reports them back to the developers for consideration. PEX uses a mix of heuristics and static code analysis to generate tests, and can even prioritize paths based on coverage information to produce the most thorough test suite possible.

Remember, in actual scenarios, we may need more complex set up and configuration to use PEX effectively. So, understanding PEX and how it works is key. Hope this helps. Good luck with Pex :)

