It appears that some method (e.g., GetContract) caused the Pex engine to execute the method under test multiple times with different inputs to verify all possible execution paths. However, this does not seem to be the specific issue here. The same issues were also reported for other methods with corresponding inputs.
For example, the "GetContract" method with the inputs "[TestMethod] [ExpectedException] public void PUT_GetContract(MyType param1, object param2, string param3)" threw an exception and an exception was thrown when the execution was re-run after the exception was thrown. 

This issue might be caused by passing objects of type "object" or "string" as an argument to the method under test. The Pex engine attempts to pass different values to these parameters, but since "object" and "string" have numerous possible values, it takes an extremely long time to run all these test cases and eventually gives up. 

To fix this, consider narrowing down the scope of your tests such that your test method under test only needs to interact with a minimal set of inputs. 

Furthermore, if these "param1" or "param2" parameters must be "object" or "string" to interact with your ArrayList implementation or API implementation, as an alternative approach, you could consider using an interface rather than the class in your API implementations. That would significantly reduce the number of possible inputs and hence make the execution more manageable for the Pex engine. 

Another approach is to use a more advanced testing library which provides better support for array or object testing, for example, Resharper's Assume package or Custom Contracts. 

Unfortunately, due to the lack of specific information, this advice applies to a broad range of possible issues, and these suggestions might not work for your case.

